        <h2>Summary / Description</h2>
        <div class="description">
          OBJECTIVES:<br><div>Wayfinding is the cognitive phenomenon that allows humans to orient and navigate in spatial surroundings. Development of a sense of direction and location is dependent upon integration of visual, proprioceptive, and vestibular information. When this information is changed or lacking, wayfinding will be affected. Studies have shown that the microgravity environment of space significantly changes both proprioceptive and vestibular responses of astronauts causing motion sickness, perceptual illusions, and spatial disorientation until adaptation occurs. However, the behavioral and neurological effects following spaceflight are unknown.</div><div><br>
</div><div>
In this investigation, the primary objective is to accomplish a comprehensive assessment of the cognitive and neurological changes that occur during long-duration spaceflight and during postflight re-adaptation to Earth’s atmosphere. The specific objectives of this investigation are to:
</div><ol>
<li> Quantify the changes in spatial orientation skills that result from long-duration spaceflight;</li>
<li> Assess functional and structural neurological changes associated with altered spatial orientation skills resulting from long-duration spaceflight;</li>
<li> Evaluate if changes associated with long-duration spaceflight persist after long-term re-adaptation to terrestrial environments.</li>
</ol>
<div>
APPROACH:<br>Data collection will focus on three measures including behavioral measures, neuroimaging, and neuropsychological measures.
</div><div>
Behavioral measures require completion of a computerized battery of tests intended to characterize figural- and environmental-scale spatial abilities. The tests include the Santa Barbara Sense of Direction Scale (SBSOD), the Spatial Configuration Task (SCT), the Path Integration Task, the Four Mountains Task, and the Mental Rotation Task.</div><div><br>
</div><div>
Neuroimaging will consist of structural and functional neuroimaging measures including anatomical MRI, diffusion tensor imaging (DTI), resting state functional MRI (rs-fMRI), task evoked functional MRI (task-fMRI), and susceptibility-weighted imaging MRI (SWI).
</div><div>
Neuropsychological measures will consist of a series of tests assessing a variety of cognitive functions, including executive function, attention, perception, memory, and mental imagery skills. The tests included are the Groton Maze Learning Test, the Set-Shifting Test, the Detection Test, the Identification Test, the One Card Learning Test, the Continuous Paired Associate Learning Test, the Delayed Recall Groton Maze Learning Test, the International Shopping List Test, the One-Back Test, the Two-Back Test, the Social-Emotional Cognition Test, and the Delayed Recall International Shopping List.
</div><div><br>
</div><div>One preflight data collection and three postflight data collections are planned. There will be no inflight data collections.
</div><div><br>
</div><div>Preflight data collection will take place between launch day minus 210 days (L-210) and L-7 and will include all three measures.
</div><div><br>
</div><div>The first postflight data collection is an abbreviated data collection planned to take place between return to Earth plus 3 days (R+3) and R+5, with flexibility to extend to R+6. Only two of the tests included in the behavioral measures will be performed. They are the Spatial Configuration Test and the Path Integration Test. The neuroimaging and neuropsychological measures will not be performed.
</div><div><br>
</div><div>The second postflight data collection will take place between R+14 and R+21. All three measures will be performed.
</div><div><br>
</div><div>The final postflight data collection will take place between R+150 and R+210. All three measures will be performed.
</div><div><br>
</div><div>RESULTS:<br> Results for this investigation are unavailable. This is an international experiment. NASA does not currently have an agreement with international space partners to archive their data in the LSDA.
</div><div></div>
